f:Ta26,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What does 'Advaita' mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Advaita means 'not-two' in Sanskrit. It asserts that ultimate reality (Brahman) is non-dual — there is only one consciousness, and the apparent multiplicity of the world is a superimposition upon it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who founded Advaita Vedanta?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Advaita Vedanta was systematized by Adi Shankaracharya (788–820 CE), though its roots are in the Upanishads. Shankaracharya wrote commentaries on the Upanishads, Bhagavad Gita, and Brahma Sutras."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is Maya in Advaita?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Maya is the power that makes the infinite, undivided Brahman appear as a universe of separate objects. It is not illusion in the Western sense (which implies nonexistence), but superimposition — like seeing a snake on a rope."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Advaita the same as nihilism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Advaita does not say the world doesn't exist. It says the world exists relatively (Vyavaharika Satta) but not ultimately (Paramarthika Satta). You are still responsible for your actions within the relative world."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does Advaita explain suffering?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Suffering arises from Avidya — ignorance of one's true nature. When we identify with the body-mind instead of the underlying awareness (Atman), we experience fear, desire, and suffering. Self-knowledge removes this root cause."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the practice of Advaita?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The primary practice is Atma Vichara — self-inquiry. Asking 'Who am I?' and tracing the feeling of 'I' back to its source until only pure awareness remains. Ramana Maharshi was its greatest modern exemplar."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Advaita similar to Buddhism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They share the idea that the fixed, separate self is not ultimately real. But Advaita asserts an underlying pure consciousness (Brahman/Atman), while Buddhism's Anatta doctrine rejects any permanent self."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is 'Aham Brahmasmi'?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"One of the four Mahavakyas (great sayings) of the Upanishads: 'I am Brahman.' It is the direct declaration of Advaita's central insight — the individual awareness and the cosmic awareness are the same."}}]}7:["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"slug":"advaita-vedanta-explained","pillar":"ancient-wisdom"}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$f"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"Article\",\"headline\":\"Advaita Vedanta Explained Simply for Western Minds\",\"description\":\"Advaita Vedanta teaches that your deepest self and ultimate reality are one. A clear, jargon-free explanation of non-dualism, Maya, Brahman, and the path to self-realization.\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/advaita-vedanta-explained\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/advaita-vedanta-explained\",\"datePublished\":\"2026-03-04\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-03-04\",\"articleSection\":\"Ancient Wisdom\",\"keywords\":[\"advaita vedanta explained\"],\"about\":[{\"@type\":\"Thing\",\"name\":\"advaita vedanta explained\"}],\"isPartOf\":{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"name\":\"Ancient Wisdom\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/ancient-wisdom-philosophies\"},\"inLanguage\":\"en\",\"author\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\"},\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com\"}}"}}],"$L10","$L11","$L12"]}],["$L13","$L14"],"$L15"]}]
